      "text": "### UNIT VI NISQ and Hardware\n\n18. Quantum Complexity and BQP, P, NP; BQP; relationship between complexity classes; implications\n\nof Shor's algorithm\n\n19. NISQ Era: Challenges and Error Mitigation, NISQ limitations; error mitigation -measurement\n\ncorrection, ZNE. Density Matrix\n\n20. Hybrid quantum-classical approach; VQE for ground state energy; QAOA for optimization;\n\nParameterized circuits\n\n21. Superconducting and Trapped Ions, Physical realization of qubits; principles, pros/cons of major\n\nplatforms.\n\n22. Photonics and Other Approaches, Photonic qubits, topological qubits, quantum annealing -D-\n\nWave.\n\n23. Quantum Error Correction -QEC - bit-flip, phase-flip codes; stabilizer codes intro -e.g., Steane\n\ncode conceptual\n\n24. Application areas -chemistry, materials, finance, ML; ethical considerations; future trends.\n\nRecitation / Tutorial Topics\n\nGuided Tutorial Activities Only\n\nTutorial 1\n\nQuantum States and Linear Algebra: Represent qubits using Dirac notation; verify normalization and\n\northogonality; compute tensor products for 2- and 3-qubit systems; identify basis states and\n\nsuperposition states.\n\nTutorial 2\n\nQuantum Gates and Circuit Design: Apply Pauli (X, Y, Z), Hadamard (H), S, and T gates on qubits;\n\ndesign simple quantum circuits; predict measurement outcomes; construct Bell states using H and\n\nCNOT gates.\n\nTutorial 3\n\nGrover's Search and Quantum Fourier Transform: Design Grover's oracle and diffusion operator;\n\nperform one and two Grover iterations; construct QFT circuits for 2- and 3-qubit systems; analyze\n\ncomputational complexity and applications.\n\nTutorial 4\n\nQuantum Phase Estimation and Shor's Algorithm: Build the QPE circuit; estimate eigenvalues of\n\nsimple unitary operators; explain order finding in Shor's algorithm; solve small integer factorization\n\nexamples conceptually.\n\nTutorial 5\n\nNISQ Computing and Hybrid Quantum Algorithms: implement parameterized quantum circuits;\n\ndemonstrate VQE and QAOA workflows using Qiskit; compare ideal and noisy simulations\n\nLab / Product Components\n\nProduct 1: Quantum Circuit Designer and Simulator\n\nObjectives: Design and simulate fundamental quantum circuits using Qiskit.\n\nKey Topics Covered: Qubits, quantum states, single- and multi-qubit gates, Bell states,\n\nmeasurements, QuantumCircuit\n\nExpected Deliverable: A Qiskit application that creates, visualizes, and simulates quantum circuits\n\nwith measurement outcomes and state visualizations.\n\nProduct 2: Quantum Algorithm Demonstrator\n\nObjectives: Implement and compare fundamental quantum algorithms with classical approaches.\n\nKey Topics Covered: Deutsch-Jozsa, Bernstein-Vazirani, Simon's Algorithm, Grover's Search, QFT\n\nExpected Deliverable: A Qiskit-based toolkit that executes selected quantum algorithms, displays",
